--TEST--
The one thing the front end accepts and nothing below it enforces is refused at its own site — a `spawn script` option is `E0777` (`rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`, goal `governance`) — while `require` is whole in both of `rule:statements/require-is-the-only-inclusion-construct`'s forms and reports nothing at either
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed`'s value form runs now: the site calls the target file's own
// script frame and keeps what its file-scope `return` handed back, typed
// `mixed` because it cannot be known statically. `nvs-ir`'s known gap 22 is
// closed and `E0704` is retired with it — no diagnostic below names this
// line, which is what this case still asserts about it.
mixed $c = require "cfg.nvs";
echo $c as string;

// The statement form is the same call with its value discarded, and the
// class the target declares is reachable from here either way: declarations
// cross a `require` (§ *Decision*).
require "cfg.nvs";
echo Cfg::name();

// `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`'s spawn itself lowers now (`E0703` is retired). What is still
// refused is an option this compiler parses and does not enforce: a `grants:`
// narrowing accepted and dropped would hand the child the parent's authority,
// so it is refused where it is written rather than ignored.
spawn script "cfg.nvs" with(grants: 7);
--FILE cfg.nvs--
<?nvs
class Cfg {
    public static function name(): string {
        return "cfg";
    }
}
return "cfg-value";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0777]: `spawn script`'s `grants:` is not enforced yet
   --> %s:20:29
   |
20 | spawn script "cfg.nvs" with(grants: 7);
   |                             ^^^^^^^^^ this option would be accepted and ignored
   = help: `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing` specifies all five options and this compiler enforces `args:` and `output:`. Refusing the other three is deliberate: a `grants:` narrowing that were silently dropped would hand the child the parent's authority

